    ":question/id": "5831f44c-02c6-5a5b-a9e7-58c7ac36cb6d",
    ":question/math-academy-id": "e-6112",
    ":question/problem": "A rocket is at a distance of $r(t)$ meters from the ground $t$ seconds after it is launched. What are the units of $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}r}{\\textrm{d}t}?$",
    ":question/worked-solution": "We have that the height $r(t)$ is measured in meters and the time is measured in seconds. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t): & \\text{meters} \\\\ t: & \\text{seconds}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}r}{\\textrm{d}t}$ measures a change in $r(t)$ over a change in $t.$ So, it represents a ratio of the units:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}: & \\frac{\\text{meters}}{\\text{seconds}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}r}{\\textrm{d}t}$ is measured in \"meters per second\"."
  },
