      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-110040",
      ":question/problem": "A website receives $v(h)$ visits every $h$ hours. What are the units of $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}h}?$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "We have that the time $h$ is measured in hours and the number of visits is measured in visitors. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h: & \\text{hours} \\\\ v(h): & \\text{visitors}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}h}$ measures a change in $v$ over a change in $h.$ So, it represents a ratio of the units:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}h}: & \\frac{\\text{visitors}}{\\text{hours}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn other words, $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}h}$ is measured in \"visitors per hour \"."
    },
