      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-72091",
      ":question/problem": "A student's parents promise to reward her $R(p)$ dollars if she obtains an average of $p$ points in her exams. What are the units of $\\frac{\\text{d}R}{\\text{d}p}?$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "We have that the student's reward $R(p)$ is measured in dollars and the average exam result is measured in points. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(p): & \\text{dollars} \\\\ p: & \\text{points}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative $\\frac{\\text{d}R}{\\text{d}p}$ measures a change in $R(p)$ over a change in $p.$ So, it represents a ratio of the units:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}R}{\\text{d}p}: & \\frac{\\text{dollars}}{\\text{points}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn other words, $\\frac{\\text{d}R}{\\text{d}p}$ is measured in \"dollars per point\"."
    },
