      ":question/id": "77542117-c7f0-50c4-8cf0-8d65753dc3a2",
      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-110037",
      ":question/problem": "Mary bakes $c$ cupcakes using $f(c)$ grams of flour. What are the units of $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}c}?$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "We have that the amount of flour $f(c)$ is measured in grams and the number of cupcakes is measured in cupcakes. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(c): & \\text{grams} \\\\ c: & \\text{cupcakes}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}c}$ measures a change in $f(c)$ over a change in $c.$ So, it represents a ratio of the units:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}c}: & \\frac{\\text{grams}}{\\text{cupcake}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}c}$ is measured in \"grams per cupcake\"."
