      ":question/id": "cc012d6e-17d6-5b41-813e-869311eeed2b",
      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-110038",
      ":question/problem": "A dairy farm sells milk on a particular day for $p(v)$ dollars, where $v$ is the number of liters of milk in the dairy farm at the start of the day. What are the units of $\\frac{\\text{d}p}{\\text{d}v}?$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "We have that the price $p(v)$ is measured in dollars and the volume of milk is measured in liters. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(v): & \\text{dollars} \\\\ v: & \\text{liters}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative $\\frac{\\text{d}p}{\\text{d}v}$ measures a change in $p(v)$ over a change in $v.$ So, it represents a ratio of the units:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}p}{\\text{d}v}: & \\frac{\\text{dollars}}{\\text{liters}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn other words, $\\frac{\\text{d}p}{\\text{d}v}$ is measured in \"dollars per liter\"."
    },
