      ":question/id": "986c7828-ebef-5b92-b3ca-04f414e27f95",
      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-110042",
      ":question/problem": "A battery lasts $L(t)$ hours when it is charged for $t$ minutes. What are the units of $\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}t}?$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "We have that the battery duration $L(t)$ is measured in hours and the charging time is measured in minutes. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(t): & \\text{hours} \\\\ t: & \\text{minutes}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative $\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}t}$ measures a change in $L(t)$ over a change in $t.$ So, it represents a ratio of the units:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}t}: & \\frac{\\text{hours}}{\\text{minute}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn other words, $\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}t}$ is measured in \"hours per minute\"."
    },
