      ":question/id": "13af356c-d230-584b-ad7a-58e4b56fa385",
      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-72092",
      ":question/problem": "A bank exchanges $D$ dollars for $E(D)$ euros. What are the units of $\\frac{\\text{d}E}{\\text{d}D}?$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "We have that the currency $E(D)$ is measured in euros and the currency $D$ is measured in dollars. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E(D): & \\text{euros} \\\\ D: & \\text{dollars}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative $\\frac{\\text{d}E}{\\text{d}D}$ measures a change in $E(D)$ over a change in $D.$ So, it represents a ratio of the units:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}E}{\\text{d}D}: & \\frac{\\text{euros}}{\\text{dollars}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn other words, $\\frac{\\text{d}E}{\\text{d}D}$ is measured in \"euros per dollar\"."
